  "title": "Days, Months, and Seasons",
  "summary": "Learn how to talk about days, months, and seasons in English. Practice using them to tell time and make plans.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "h": "Days of the Week",
      "p": "There are seven days in a week. We use capital letters for all days like Monday and Tuesday. We say 'on' before a day, for example, 'on Monday'. The weekend is Saturday and Sunday.",
      "ex": [
        {
          "en": "Today is Monday.",
          "mean": "This is the day today."
        },
        {
          "en": "I work on Tuesday.",
          "mean": "My work day is Tuesday."
        },
        {
          "en": "We rest on Sunday.",
          "mean": "Sunday is a day of rest."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "h": "Months of the Year",
      "p": "There are twelve months in a year. The year starts in January and ends in December. We also use capital letters for months. We use 'in' before a month, like 'in January'.",
      "ex": [
        {
          "en": "My birthday is in January.",
          "mean": "The month of my birth is January."
        },
        {
          "en": "School starts in September.",
          "mean": "The school year begins in September."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "h": "The Four Seasons",
      "p": "A year has four seasons: spring, summer, autumn, and winter. Each season brings different weather. We use 'in' before seasons, such as 'in summer'.",
      "ex": [
        {
          "en": "It is hot in summer.",
          "mean": "Summer weather is warm."
        },
        {
          "en": "It snows in winter.",
          "mean": "Winter has snow."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
